  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "How does a glacier carry rocks?",
      "a": "Rocks fall onto the glacier from valley sides, and the ice also freezes onto bedrock and plucks pieces away. These rocks travel inside, under and on top of the moving ice.",
      "asks": [
        "What does a glacier carry?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What is a moraine?",
      "a": "A moraine is a ridge or mound of rock and soil left by a glacier. End moraines form at the glacier's snout, and lateral moraines form along its sides.",
      "asks": [
        "What is an end moraine?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why are there huge boulders in odd places?",
      "a": "Many are glacial erratics: boulders carried by ice age glaciers, sometimes hundreds of kilometres, and dropped when the ice melted. They often differ from the local rock.",
      "asks": [
        "What is a glacial erratic?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What is till?",
      "a": "Till is the unsorted mix of clay, sand, gravel and boulders dumped directly by melting glacier ice.",
      "asks": [
        "What is boulder clay?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What is a drumlin?",
      "a": "A drumlin is a smooth, oval hill of till shaped by a glacier flowing over it, like an upside-down spoon. Its steep end points the way the ice came from.",
      "asks": [
        "What shape is a drumlin?"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
    "Glacial erratics can be carried hundreds of kilometres from their source rock.",
    "Long Island in New York is largely made of moraines left by ice age glaciers."
  ],
